---
import DocumentationLayout from '../../layouts/DocumentationLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../components/sections/CardGrid.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ClosingCTA from '../../components/sections/ClosingCTA.astro';
import { getDocsBySection } from '../../utils/collections';
import { buildCollectionPageJsonLD } from '../../utils/seo';

const useCases = await getDocsBySection('use-cases');
const cards = useCases.map((doc) => ({
  title: doc.data.doc_title,
  description: doc.data.description,
  href: `/use-cases/${doc.id}`,
}));

const jsonLD = buildCollectionPageJsonLD({
  title: 'Use Cases',
  description: 'Illustrative aDNA adoption scenarios across solo developers, startups, research labs, and enterprises.',
  url: Astro.url.href,
});
---

<DocumentationLayout
  title="Use Cases"
  description="Illustrative aDNA adoption scenarios across solo developers, startups, research labs, and enterprises."
  jsonLD={jsonLD}
>
  {/* HAUSSMANN P4.2 O3 — F13/F19 thin-hub treatment (was h2=1, bodyLen 1,530). The honesty
       framing was already the best thing on this page; it was just doing its work inside a Callout
       with no section around it. Structuring it makes it findable rather than incidental. */}
  <p>See how different kinds of teams and individuals could use aDNA to organize their work. Each scenario follows an <strong>illustrative</strong> persona through a concrete deployment.</p>

  <h2 id="illustrative-not-case-studies">Illustrative, not case studies</h2>
  <Callout variant="info" title="Illustrative scenarios">
    <p>These personas are composite archetypes, not real named adopters — they show the shape of a deployment, not a case study. For real, cited subnetworks running on aDNA today, see <a href="/commons/">the commons</a>.</p>
  </Callout>
  <p>The distinction is load-bearing and we would rather lose the persuasive version of this page than blur it. A case study reports what someone did; a scenario shows what a deployment looks like. Only one of those is evidence, and pages that quietly present the second as the first are the reason the first is worth anything.</p>

  <h2 id="how-to-read-a-scenario">How to read one</h2>
  <p>Each scenario names a starting situation, the vaults the persona ends up with, and the decisions that got them there. The useful part is usually the <em>shape</em> — how many vaults, where the boundaries fall, what stays local — because that is the part that transfers. The specific tools and counts will not match your situation and are not meant to.</p>
  <p>If none of them fits, that is a legitimate outcome rather than a gap in your understanding: aDNA is a structure for organising context, and plenty of real deployments look like none of these four.</p>

  <h2 id="the-scenarios">The scenarios</h2>
  <CardGrid cards={cards} columns={2} />

  {/* Storyweave P3 / M3.2 — wire the on-ramp: the scenarios are illustrative, but the path is real.
      No browse-only dead-end — every reader can start their own vault or find their adopter lane. */}
  <ClosingCTA
    title="See yourself in one of these?"
    lead="The scenarios are illustrative — the path is real. Start your own vault, or read the standard it is built on."
    primaryLabel="Get started"
    primaryHref="/get-started/"
    secondaryLabel="Read the standard"
    secondaryHref="/reference/"
  />
</DocumentationLayout>
